WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.073
(audience applauding)

00:00:01.073 --> 00:00:01.906
- All right.

00:00:01.906 --> 00:00:02.739
And we have someone very special

00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:03.990
who requested that song in particular.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.340
He is the host of "Adam Eats the 80s".

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.467
Give it up for Adam Richmond.

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:09.300
(audience cheering)

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.461
(guitar music)

00:00:12.461 --> 00:00:13.711
What's up Adam?

00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:16.290
- What's up Kelly?

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.123
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:19.980
I brought you my country
western glitz and glamor.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.420
Do you see my shoulder pads, dude?

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.816
I look so-

00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:25.213
- [Adam] You look
amazing, you look amazing.

00:00:25.213 --> 00:00:26.353
- I look so good with my shoulder pads.

00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:27.699
(audience cheering)

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:29.850
We're gonna talk about your-

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.400
- Chef's kiss.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.233
- Thank you.

00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:33.890
We're gonna talk about
your show in a second,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.530
but first why do you love that song?

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.363
- My dad.

00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:39.670
This is actually a
picture of him right here.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.810
My dad may he rest in peace,
was a big country fan.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.150
My first concert was the Statler Brothers.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:45.983
- [Kelly] Oh wow.

00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:50.040
- And I grew up hearing him
play Ronnie Milsap songs.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.040
I think people nowadays don't
realize how popular he was,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.076
but from like 74 to 78

00:00:56.076 --> 00:00:57.900
he had something like
seven number one hits.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.990
And so I've always loved his music.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.467
I've always loved him.

00:01:02.845 --> 00:01:04.086
(audience applauds)

00:01:04.086 --> 00:01:05.779
- And it's just really
special to hear you sing it.

00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:06.612
- I love it.

00:01:06.612 --> 00:01:08.150
He could've named that
smokey mountain rain too.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.760
I'm just saying there's
another song there.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.160
But tell us about your new show.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.010
It's about food in the 80's, right?

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.030
- Correct. So "Adam Eats
the 80s" is looking back

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.380
at this incredibly dynamic and energetic

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.950
and incredible decade through the prism

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.560
of food and looking at your audience.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.070
They already know the fashion was amazing.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.770
The fads, the big hair,
the big shoulder pads,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.790
but people don't realize

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.060
that it changed American food forever.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.940
There were brands that
emerged that became national

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.430
and global icons.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:42.430
And it also, between the
invention of the microwave,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.330
between fast food and
drive thrus proliferating,

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.600
between Reagan saying you could
advertise directly to kids.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.310
So you sugar cereals
with video game scenes

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:54.300
- So wait, I have a question though.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.863
Wait, do you have like
a favorite 80s candy?

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.590
- Yes. There's so many candies I love,

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.590
but I think that in '84,
Nerds was actually voted Candy

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.373
of the Year.

00:02:07.373 --> 00:02:08.206
(audience cheering)

00:02:08.206 --> 00:02:11.810
And people don't realize
how fascinating the story

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.130
behind Nerds was.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:13.990
This was the company

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.450
that had licensed the Willy Wonka brand.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.800
And one scientist saw the little bits

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.330
of Everlasting Gobstopper in
the bottom of the machine.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.680
And he said, let's, let's coat
these little hardened bits

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.610
of sweetened sugar with ascorbic acid.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.443
Make 'em sour.

00:02:29.443 --> 00:02:30.743
Make 'em fruity.

00:02:30.743 --> 00:02:31.576
- [Kelly] What?!

00:02:31.576 --> 00:02:33.530
- And the Everlasting
Gobstopper became the Nerd,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.840
and those little designs on the package.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.290
I think I sent you guys some.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:39.640
Those little designs,

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.360
the graphic designer used
to doodle them on his desk,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.503
and they just decided, hey,
let's just make these the logo.

00:02:47.408 --> 00:02:49.324
- This is brand new information.

00:02:49.324 --> 00:02:51.490
(audience applauds)

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:55.378
I did not know that that's what Nerds was.

00:02:55.378 --> 00:02:56.211
I love Nerds.

00:02:56.211 --> 00:02:57.780
- In fact, one of the scientists,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.613
They're so good.

00:02:58.613 --> 00:03:01.140
One of the scientists'
kids actually named them.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.010
So you can't blame it.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:04.843
(audience cheering)

00:03:04.843 --> 00:03:05.691
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:05.691 --> 00:03:06.524
All right.

00:03:06.524 --> 00:03:07.357
Well, wait, we have a clip

00:03:07.357 --> 00:03:08.440
of "Adam eats the 80s".

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.570
Take a look.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.058
- This is what I do for you.

00:03:11.058 --> 00:03:12.407
(guitar track)

00:03:12.407 --> 00:03:16.070
The 1980s was a deliciously doughy decade.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.790
You were either making dough,
baking dough or eating it.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.030
Whether it was Auntie
Anne's decision to expand

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.900
into malls and stick
with their recipe for all

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.810
of these years or Domino's
attempting to cut a slice

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.790
of the breakfast market
with a pizza gone too soon.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.730
The echoes of the 80s
can still be heard today.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.938
And some of them are really, really tasty.

00:03:38.938 --> 00:03:40.800
- Thank you so much, Adam.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:41.811
I love you.

00:03:41.811 --> 00:03:42.662
(audience cheers)

00:03:42.662 --> 00:03:43.662
Come back on my show.

00:03:43.662 --> 00:03:44.562
- I love you, too.

